            "attachment_id": {},
            "count": 0,
            "bug_id": 19315,
            "text": "Every dispatch through UseMethod() keeps two error-message buffers on the C stack while the method runs:\n\n- char buf[8192] in dispatchMethod(), used only for the R_USEMETHOD_FORWARD_LOCALS=error message;\n- char cl[1024] in do_usemethod(), used only for the \"no applicable method\" error.\n\nThat is about 70% of the C stack one level of S3 recursion uses, so recursion through an S3 method is the shallowest kind in R.\n\nReproducible example: usemethod-stack-repro.R, attached, prints the depth reached before \"C stack usage is too close to the limit\". With an 8 MB stack on macOS arm64 (r90888, clang) it is 607 through UseMethod() against 1959 through do.call(); with the patch it is 2117.\n\nProposed patch: attached as usemethod-stack-buffer.diff, against r90888. It moves each buffer, with the code that uses it, into a helper kept out of line by a new R_NOINLINE macro in Defn.h (the counterpart of R_INLINE). The frames of dispatchMethod() and do_usemethod() shrink from 8368 and 1168 bytes to 144 and 112; the code is otherwise unchanged.\n\nmake check passes, and the error messages and the R_USEMETHOD_FORWARD_LOCALS settings behave as before. The patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/491, where CI results can be seen.",
